<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/advanced/economic-and-political-anthropology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Economics assumes the market; political science assumes the state. Anthropology is the
    discipline that studies the rest of the human record — and most of it ran on neither. The
    comparative evidence (gift economies, segmentary polities, multiple value spheres, states
    experienced from below) shows both market and state to be historical designs with
    preconditions and costs, not human nature. That makes this chapter a counter-laboratory: every
    universal claim in economics or political science ("rational maximisers", "no order without a
    sovereign") meets here its empirical edge cases.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the gift, embeddedness, object biographies, order without states" widget="equationStep" config={econanthCfg} />

  <h2>The gift: exchange as social creation</h2>
  <p>
    Mauss's <em>The Gift</em> (1925) is the discipline's most consequential essay. Surveying
    potlatch, kula and Roman and Germanic law, he found no "pure" gifts: giving, receiving and
    reciprocating are obligatory, and the obligation is the point — gifts weave <strong>total
    social systems</strong>, binding giver and receiver across time. The Trobriand <strong>kula
    ring</strong> (Malinowski's data) is the classic case: dangerous open-sea voyages to exchange
    shell necklaces and armbands that circulate for decades, conferring fame rather than utility —
    with practical trade riding along in its wake, as gift-relationships create the trust that
    commerce needs. Sahlins (<em>Stone Age Economics</em>) systematised the logic: <strong>reciprocity
    shades with social distance</strong> — generalised (give without accounting: kin), balanced
    (even, timed exchange: trade partnerships), negative (get the better: market haggling at its
    coldest). What you may expect back <em>is</em> who someone is to you — exchange is a social
    map continuously redrawn. Note the convergence: behavioural economics ultimatum-game refusals
    of "unfair" offers look, from this side, like Mauss's obligated reciprocators — two traditions
    arriving at the same human from opposite directions.
  </p>

  <h2>Embeddedness and the substantivist-formalist debate</h2>
  <p>
    Polanyi (<em>The Great Transformation</em>) supplied the historical frame: pre-modern
    economies were <strong>embedded</strong> — production and distribution organised by kinship,
    reciprocity, redistribution (temple and chiefly storage) and householding — while the
    <strong>self-regulating market</strong>, with land, labour and money treated as commodities, is
    a recent, deliberately institutionalised design, and a fragile one (the double movement of the
    sociology globalisation lesson is his). Anthropology's version of the argument was the
    <strong>substantivist-formalist debate</strong>: formalists (applying neoclassical optimisation
    to any economy) versus substantivists (economies as culturally particular institutions). The
    productive residue: allocate formal analysis where it fits (peasant market behaviour fits
    optimisation models surprisingly well — the rational peasant is a real finding, not an insult),
    while insisting that prices, property and even "profit" are institutionally constituted.
    The <strong>division of labour</strong> follows the same lesson: specialisation by age, gender,
    kin group and craft (the industrial lesson's specialisation, re-embedded) is universal, but who
    may do what is a moral and political arrangement — "women's work," hereditary crafts, caste
    occupations — not a market-clearing outcome.
  </p>

  <h2>Commodification: spheres, biographies, blocked exchanges</h2>
  <p>
    Money is not culturally neutral. Bohannan's <strong>Tiv spheres of exchange</strong> sorted
    transactions into ranked, morally bounded categories — subsistence goods, marriagewealth
    (brass rods), prestige (cattle, office titles) — with conversion between spheres fraught and
    colonial currency's arrival collapsing the boundaries in a generation, with moral chaos
    following. Kopytoff and Appadurai generalised: things have <strong>social biographies</strong>,
    moving through regimes of value — a car becomes a sacred relic becomes scrap; the question an
    ethnographer asks of any object is where it sits and how it crossed. And every society runs
    <strong>blocked exchanges</strong> — categories of thing its currency may not buy: votes,
    honours, human beings (formally, post-abolition), blood (Titmuss's comparison: paid US blood
    systems produced worse supply and safety than voluntary donation — the market crowding out the
    gift), sexual access, kidneys. The anthropological point is not anti-market but anti-
    universalist: <em>which</em> things are commodifiable is a society's ongoing moral settlement,
    and redesigning money redesigns the settlement.
  </p>

  <h2>Political organisation: order without the state</h2>
  <p>
    The typology (Service's sequence) orders the comparative record: <strong>bands</strong>
    (egalitarian foragers, leadership by achievement and example, conflict managed by mobility and
    fission), <strong>tribes</strong> (segmentary lineages and age-sets, "big man" achievement),
    <strong>chiefdoms</strong> (hereditary hierarchy, redistribution from stored surplus,
    monumental labour), <strong>states</strong> (centralised monopoly of legitimate force, law,
    bureaucracy). The intellectual shock is the first three: Evans-Pritchard's Nuer — acephalous,
    feuding, and orderly — showed <strong>order without government</strong>: segmentary opposition
    ("I against my brother; my brother and I against my cousin; we against the stranger") fusing
    nested groups against common threats and balancing violence without a sovereign — Hobbes's
    stateless "war of all against all" meets its counterexample, and the curse, the feud and the
    compensatory payment (currently operational in stateless Somali regions) stand as working
    <strong>legal systems</strong> — social control through moral force, liability and ritual
    rather than police and courts. Weber's three authority types (traditional, charismatic,
    legal-rational) give the comparative anchor; the big-man/chief contrast gives its dynamic:
    authority built by generosity (and endlessly contestable) versus authority built by office
    (and backed by force).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "no state means chaos, and chiefs are just primitive kings."
    Stateless societies ran sophisticated conflict regulation for millennia, and the chiefdom's
    defining feature — redistribution and ascribed rank — is precisely what big-man systems
    <em>lack</em>: the big man must re-earn position continuously by giving; the chief inherits
    it. The differences are structural, not developmental stages to condescend to.
  </p>

  <h2>Power from below: ideology, resistance, nation-building</h2>
  <p>
    Political anthropology reads power from the ground. Scott's <strong>weapons of the weak</strong>
    (Malaysian villages, 1985): where open rebellion is suicidal, the dominated resist everyday and
    deniably — foot-dragging, false compliance, evasion, slander, theft — forms that leave no
    manifesto and, precisely for that reason, elude state archives; his later <em>Seeing Like a
    State</em> (1998) turned the lens on the state itself: high-modernist schemes (scientific
    forestry, collective farms, Brasília) simplify society into legibility — surnames, cadastral
    maps, monocultures, standardised language — making populations governable and plans auditable,
    while destroying the local knowledge (mētis) that made systems work; grand schemes fail,
    structurally, when legibility is purchased at the price of function. <strong>Ideology</strong>
    is studied as lived — rituals, rumours, witchcraft accusations as the idiom in which unequal
    power gets argued about (witchcraft flourishing where accumulation appears morally illegitimate:
    a social critique of inequality wearing supernatural dress). <strong>Nation-building</strong>
    from below is the same story at scale: census categories, maps, schools and monuments
    manufacture the nation Hobsbawm called invented tradition — and the anthropological record
    documents both the manufacture and the messy local receptions that decide whether it sticks.
    Development and industrialisation get the same treatment: proletarianisation (separating people
    from means of production — enclosure's global repetition), urbanisation (kinship networks
    rebuilt in cities rather than dissolved — the urban village is the robust finding),
    and the development encounter itself, where projects designed in one ontology are implemented
    in another ( Ferguson's <em>Anti-Politics Machine</em>: development discourse depoliticising
    structural questions into technical ones).
  </p>

  <h2>Households, gender and the economy from below</h2>
  <p>
    The household is where economic anthropology's method shows its difference from
    economics. Households are not unitary maximisers: they are sites of bargaining, and the
    intra-household allocation literature shows resources behaving differently depending on
    who controls them — worldwide evidence finds income in women's hands shifting spending
    toward children's food, health and schooling, a finding that demolishes the unitary model
    and changes development policy instrument by instrument (cash transfers to mothers; the
    microcredit focus on women's groups). Time allocation is the hidden economy: unpaid care
    and subsistence work — predominantly women's — sustains the monetised economy and
    vanishes from GDP; time-use surveys built it back into view and reframed "labour" and
    "leisure" across the development literature. And the moral economy of the household — who
    owes whom, what may be sold, what must be guarded — is culturally specific and politically
    loaded. The feminist-economic-anthropology synthesis (gender as structure, not variable)
    is one of the subfield's most consequential exports to policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Consumption, industrialisation and the global encounter</h2>
  <p>
    Consumption is meaning-making, not just acquiring: Douglas and Isherwood's <em>The World
    of Goods</em> read consumption as communication (goods draw social boundaries), Miller's
    ethnographies of shopping and material culture found love and sacrifice inside everyday
    purchases, and studies of secondhand markets — the global trade in used clothing from
    charity bins to Kenyan markets — trace value's afterlives, showing "waste" as someone's
    commodity chain. Industrialisation and proletarianisation get the same treatment from
    below: the factory floor as social system (why assembly plants recruit kin networks; how
    piece rates interact with obligation), urbanisation as network-rebuilding (the African
    and Asian urban village — kinship transplanted and adapted, not dissolved), and the
    commodification debates: what happens to land, water, labour and tradition when they
    enter markets — enclosure's repeated global performances, documented locally, each with
    the losers' precise accounting. The through-line: economic change is never just prices
    moving; it is obligations, identities and categories being restructured — which is why
    the anthropologist's field site sits at the end of every supply chain and inside every
    statistics category.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Debt as social technology.</strong> Graeber's synthesis (Debt, 2011) extended Mauss:
    credit and debt precede coinage by millennia; barter-spot-markets appear mostly between
    strangers or after currency collapse; and moral economies of debt (jubilees, debt bondage,
    its abolition) track the power relations debt encodes — the historical counter-record to
    economics' barter origin story. <strong>Moral economy.</strong> E.P. Thompson's concept,
    naturalised in anthropology: subsistence ethics (the rich man in the gatehouse; the norm that
    price gouging in famine is theft) are real, enforceable institutions — peasant rebellion
    typically defends a moral economy against market logic rather than demanding a market one,
    which re-reads modern food riots and land conflicts as normative defence, not irrationality.
    <strong>The state, experienced.</strong> Following the Seeing-Like-a-State line, ethnographies
    of encounter (border posts, courts, registration offices, NGOs) document the state as a set of
    mundane practices people navigate, evade and re-appropriate — bureaucracy as a cultural system
    with its own ritual logic. <strong>Open questions:</strong> whether digital platforms
    constitute a new embedding regime (reputation systems re-creating the trust Economies of the
    village, at scale and at extraction); how value-sphere theory applies to data, attention and
    reputation as currencies; whether the deep history of political scale (Graeber and Wengrow's
    <em>The Dawn of Everything</em> argument that prehistoric societies consciously alternated
    political forms) reshapes the band-to-state sequence or merely complicates it; and how
    climate-driven scarcity will interact with segmentary and state logics of conflict — the
    comparative record is suddenly the policy-relevant one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The commons literature (Ostrom's programme) deserves explicit placement in this
    synthesis: decades of fieldwork across fisheries, forests, pastures and irrigation
    systems documented communities governing shared resources without states or
    privatisation — through boundary rules, monitoring, graduated sanctions and
    conflict-resolution forums designed locally. The finding rewrote the
    tragedy-of-the-commons certainty: commons fail under open access, not under common
    property, and governance design (institutional fit to the resource) predicts
    sustainability better than ownership category. The connection to this chapter is
    direct: commons governance is order-without-the-state at its most constructive, the
    gift's obligations and the segmentary logic's balancing scaled to resource management —
    and the design principles extracted from ethnography now shape fisheries policy, climate
    adaptation and (the literature's newest extension) digital-resource governance. It is
    the clearest case of this subfield's method changing policy at scale.
  </p>

